const themes = {
  'food': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring genuine mathematical fluency and independent reading ability to food-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect nutrition education to multi-digit arithmetic, informational writing, and critical analysis of health data. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, understand place value to the hundreds, and are beginning to grasp the foundational ideas behind multiplication and fractions. Food worksheets at this level pose problems like a recipe serves four people and uses twelve strawberries, how many strawberries would you need to serve eight people, introducing the concept of doubling and proportional reasoning through relatable kitchen math. Nutrition label reading becomes a genuine academic activity as children interpret serving sizes, calorie counts, and ingredient lists, extracting numerical data from authentic informational text. Reading passages grow to full paragraphs about topics like how the body uses different food groups, why whole grains provide lasting energy, or how food travels from farm to table, with comprehension questions that require main idea identification, supporting detail extraction, and drawing conclusions from evidence. Writing tasks ask students to compose organized paragraphs: an opinion piece about their favorite healthy snack with three supporting reasons, or an informational report about a food group that includes facts gathered from reading worksheets. Data interpretation activities present bar graphs and pictographs of cafeteria food choices and challenge children to answer multi-step questions about totals, differences, and comparisons across categories. Second graders can also engage meaningfully with the concept of balanced meals across a full day, tracking breakfast, lunch, dinner, and snacks to evaluate whether all food groups are represented. The combination of real-world nutritional relevance with second-grade academic rigor makes food worksheets a powerful cross-curricular resource that children approach with enthusiasm because the subject matter connects to choices they make every single day.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve two-step word problems within 100 involving food quantities, recipe scaling, and nutritional data', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph informational text about nutrition and food systems and identify main ideas with supporting details', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Compose organized opinion and informational paragraphs about food and healthy eating using evidence from reading', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the sustained attention to work through multi-step food problems independently for twenty to twenty-five minutes and the reading stamina to engage with full paragraphs of nutritional information. Their growing ability to think critically about health claims means they can evaluate whether a meal is truly balanced rather than simply identifying food groups, and their writing skills support organized paragraphs with topic sentences and supporting details.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Challenge students to plan a full day of balanced meals using a food group checklist, calculating total servings from each group and writing a paragraph explaining their choices and why the menu is nutritious.',\n        'Use real nutrition labels from packaged foods as worksheet supplements, asking students to compare two products and write about which is the healthier choice based on specific numerical evidence from the labels.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade food worksheets introduce multiplication concepts?', answer: 'Recipe scaling provides a natural context for multiplicative thinking. When a recipe for four people needs to feed eight, children double each ingredient quantity, practicing repeated addition that leads directly to multiplication understanding. Problems like each lunch tray has three fruit servings and there are five trays connect food scenarios to equal-group thinking.' },\n        { question: 'What informational writing skills do second-grade food worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students compose structured paragraphs about nutrition topics with a clear topic sentence, supporting facts gathered from reading passages, and a concluding statement. They also write opinion pieces about food preferences backed by reasons, building the organized multi-sentence writing that second-grade standards require across all content areas.' },\n        { question: 'How do food worksheets support second-grade data interpretation skills?', answer: 'They present bar graphs, pictographs, and tally charts showing food preference surveys or cafeteria data. Students answer multi-step questions requiring them to calculate totals, find differences between categories, and draw conclusions about patterns, connecting real nutritional data to the measurement and data strand of second-grade math standards.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'cooking': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring the mathematical fluency and reading independence needed to engage with cooking worksheets at a genuinely practical level, tackling recipe math that involves two-digit quantities, measurement conversions, and multi-paragraph procedural reading. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, understand the relationship between different measurement units, and read complex instructions independently, making them ready for cooking activities that mirror real kitchen tasks. Math worksheets at this level present problems like the recipe calls for two cups of flour but you want to make half a batch, how much flour do you need, introducing fraction concepts through the most natural context imaginable. Measurement conversion problems ask students to figure out how many tablespoons are in a quarter cup or how many cups fill a quart, building unit relationship understanding that serves both math and science curricula. Reading activities include full recipe texts with ingredient lists, numbered steps, and yield information, and comprehension questions that require students to sequence procedures, identify dependencies between steps, and evaluate what would happen if a step were skipped or performed out of order. Writing tasks challenge students to compose their own complete recipes with precise measurements, clear step-by-step instructions, and an introduction explaining what makes the dish special, developing informational and procedural writing skills simultaneously. Pattern recognition advances to time-based sequences, like understanding that bread dough needs to rise for an hour between two kneading sessions, building timeline reasoning alongside mathematical thinking. Second graders can also begin analyzing recipes critically, comparing two versions of the same dish and determining which uses more of a particular ingredient or which has more steps, developing the comparative analysis skills that support both mathematical and literary reasoning.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve measurement conversion and recipe scaling problems using addition, subtraction, and early fraction concepts', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read complete recipes as procedural text and evaluate step dependencies and sequencing logic', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write original recipes with precise measurements, sequential steps, and introductory context', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the executive function skills to manage multi-step recipe problems that require holding several quantities in working memory simultaneously. Their reading fluency supports independent engagement with full-length recipes, and their growing understanding of time allows them to reason about cooking durations, waiting periods, and scheduling multiple recipe components. Writing stamina at this age supports composing complete procedural texts with multiple paragraphs.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Have students compare two recipe versions for the same dish, creating a chart showing differences in ingredients and quantities, then writing a paragraph explaining which version they would choose and why based on the numerical evidence.',\n        'Assign a recipe scaling challenge where students must adjust a recipe that serves four to serve their entire class, requiring multiplication of every ingredient and providing authentic multi-digit computation practice.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade cooking worksheets introduce fractions?', answer: 'Recipes naturally use fractions in measurement: half a cup, a quarter teaspoon, three-fourths of a stick of butter. Worksheets present these fractional quantities in meaningful contexts where children can visualize the amounts, making abstract fraction concepts concrete. Halving and doubling recipes provides repeated practice with doubling and splitting fractional quantities.' },\n        { question: 'What procedural writing skills do cooking worksheets build at the second-grade level?', answer: 'Students write complete recipes including an ingredient list with precise measurements, numbered steps using transition words, and notes about timing or temperature. This structured procedural writing develops organization, precision, and audience awareness, as students must write clearly enough for someone else to follow their instructions successfully.' },\n        { question: 'Can second-grade cooking worksheets connect to science standards?', answer: 'Yes. Cooking involves observable physical and chemical changes: melting butter demonstrates states of matter, baking dough shows irreversible chemical change, and dissolving sugar in water illustrates solutions. Worksheets that ask students to predict and explain what happens during cooking steps build scientific observation and reasoning skills alongside culinary knowledge.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'clothing': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring multi-digit arithmetic skills, independent reading fluency, and growing analytical thinking to clothing-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect wardrobe decisions to real-world math, informational text about textiles, and organized descriptive writing. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, work with place value concepts, and write organized paragraphs, making them ready for clothing activities that go far beyond simple sorting. Math worksheets at this level present problems like a school uniform costs forty-seven dollars and a winter coat costs sixty-three dollars, how much would both cost together, or a store has eighty-five shirts and sells thirty-nine during a sale, how many remain. Price comparison activities challenge students to determine which of three outfit combinations costs the least, requiring multi-step addition and comparison of two-digit totals. Measurement enters practically through activities where students measure sleeve length, shoe size, or scarf length using rulers marked in inches and centimeters, connecting clothing to standard unit measurement skills. Reading passages explore how different fabrics are made, why certain materials are waterproof while others breathe, or how clothing styles reflect cultural traditions and climate, with comprehension questions that require identifying main ideas and supporting evidence across multiple paragraphs. Writing tasks ask students to compose opinion paragraphs about their favorite type of clothing with three supporting reasons, or informational paragraphs about how a specific fabric like cotton or wool is produced from raw material to finished garment. Pattern creation worksheets challenge students to design fabric patterns following specific mathematical rules with four or more elements, then describe the rule in writing. The combination of practical life relevance with second-grade academic expectations makes clothing worksheets a versatile resource that connects everyday choices to rigorous cross-curricular learning.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve two-step word problems within 100 involving clothing prices, quantities, and measurement comparisons', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read informational passages about textiles, cultural clothing traditions, and fabric production and summarize key ideas', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write organized opinion and informational paragraphs about clothing using descriptive vocabulary and supporting evidence', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the mathematical reasoning to compare multi-digit prices and calculate totals across multiple items, skills that clothing shopping scenarios naturally exercise. Their reading comprehension supports engaging with informational text about how things are made, and their writing has matured to the point where they can produce organized paragraphs with topic sentences, supporting details, and concluding statements about clothing topics they find personally relevant.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Set up a classroom clothing store with price tags on illustrated items and give students a budget of one hundred dollars, challenging them to plan a complete outfit while staying within budget and recording their addition work on a worksheet.',\n        'Have students research one type of fabric and write an informational paragraph explaining where it comes from, what makes it special, and which clothing items are commonly made from it, combining reading comprehension with structured writing practice.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade clothing worksheets develop money math skills?', answer: 'They present realistic shopping scenarios requiring students to add multi-digit prices, compare costs of different outfit combinations, calculate change from a given budget, and determine how much more one item costs than another. These problems use the dollar amounts and comparison reasoning that second-grade math standards emphasize within a context children find personally engaging.' },\n        { question: 'What measurement skills do second-grade clothing worksheets build?', answer: 'Students measure clothing items like scarves, ribbons, and shoe outlines using rulers marked in inches and centimeters. They compare measurements across items, record data in tables, and answer questions about which item is longest or shortest. This practical measurement with standard units directly addresses second-grade measurement and data standards.' },\n        { question: 'How do clothing worksheets support second-grade informational writing?', answer: 'They provide engaging topics for structured paragraphs: how cotton becomes a t-shirt, why we dress differently in different climates, or which fabric is best for rain. Students practice organizing ideas with topic sentences and supporting facts, building the informational writing skills that second-grade standards require across all subject areas.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'household': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring multi-digit computation skills, independent reading ability, and growing analytical thinking to household-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect home environments to measurement with standard units, informational reading about home systems, and organized descriptive writing using precise spatial vocabulary. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, measure objects using rulers and tape measures, and write structured paragraphs, making them ready for household activities that go well beyond basic room sorting. Math worksheets at this level present problems like the living room is twelve feet long and the kitchen is nine feet long, how much longer is the living room, or each bedroom has two windows and there are four bedrooms plus a living room with three windows, how many windows does the house have in total. These multi-step scenarios develop both computational fluency and the ability to extract mathematical relationships from real-world descriptions. Measurement activities become hands-on as students measure actual household objects with rulers and record data in organized tables, then compare measurements and answer analytical questions. Reading passages explore how household systems work, from how water reaches the kitchen tap to why insulation keeps homes warm in winter, with comprehension questions requiring students to identify cause-and-effect relationships and summarize main ideas across paragraphs. Writing tasks ask students to compose detailed room descriptions using at least six different prepositions, write procedural paragraphs explaining a household routine like doing laundry or setting the table, or create opinion pieces about how they would redesign a room and why. Safety education deepens as students read about and analyze emergency procedures, evaluating the reasoning behind household safety rules rather than simply memorizing them. The household theme sustains engagement for second graders because their growing independence at home means they are taking on real chores and responsibilities, making every worksheet feel directly connected to their expanding role in family life.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multi-step word problems within 100 involving household measurements, room dimensions, and object quantities', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read informational passages about household systems and safety procedures and identify cause-and-effect relationships', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write detailed descriptive and procedural paragraphs about household topics using precise spatial and sequence vocabulary', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the spatial reasoning maturity to work with actual measurements of rooms and furniture, understanding that a twelve-foot room is larger than a nine-foot room and being able to calculate the difference. Their reading comprehension supports engaging with explanatory text about how things work in the home, and their writing has progressed to the point where they can produce multi-sentence descriptions and procedural paragraphs with logical organization and appropriate transition words.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Have students create a measured floor plan of their bedroom using a tape measure, recording dimensions in feet and inches, then calculating the total distance around the room to introduce perimeter in a personally meaningful context.',\n        'Assign a household systems research project where students read about how one system works, such as plumbing or electricity, and write an informational paragraph with a diagram, building both reading comprehension and explanatory writing skills.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade household worksheets develop real-world measurement skills?', answer: 'Students measure household objects and room dimensions using rulers and tape measures marked in standard units. They record measurements in organized tables, compare lengths and widths, and solve problems involving differences between measurements. This practical application of standard unit measurement directly addresses second-grade math standards for measurement and data.' },\n        { question: 'What types of informational reading do household worksheets provide at the second-grade level?', answer: 'They include passages about how household systems like plumbing and heating work, why homes are built with specific materials, and how emergency safety procedures protect families. Comprehension questions require students to identify main ideas, trace cause-and-effect relationships, and summarize information across multiple paragraphs.' },\n        { question: 'Can household worksheets help second graders develop responsibility and life skills?', answer: 'Yes. Procedural writing about household routines like doing laundry, washing dishes, or organizing a closet builds both writing skills and practical knowledge. When students write clear step-by-step instructions for a chore, they demonstrate understanding of the task while practicing the organized procedural writing that second-grade standards emphasize.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'furniture': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring measurement fluency, multi-digit arithmetic, and growing spatial analysis skills to furniture-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect room design to area and perimeter introduction, standard unit measurement, and analytical writing about spatial arrangements. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, measure with rulers in inches and centimeters, and write organized multi-sentence paragraphs, making them ready for furniture activities that bridge everyday spatial experience with formal mathematical concepts. Math worksheets at this level introduce area and perimeter through furniture contexts: if a rectangular table top measures four feet by three feet, what is the distance around the edge, or how many square foot tiles would cover the table surface. These hands-on geometry problems make abstract measurement concepts tangible because children can visualize and even measure the actual furniture being described. Room layout activities advance to scale drawings where one square on grid paper represents one foot, and students plan furniture arrangements that must fit within specific dimensions, practicing both spatial reasoning and computational skills. Reading passages explore topics like how furniture designers plan ergonomic chairs, why different wood types are used for different purposes, or how furniture styles have changed across centuries, with comprehension questions requiring main idea identification and comparison across paragraphs. Writing tasks challenge students to compose detailed room descriptions using measurement vocabulary and at least eight spatial terms, or to write persuasive paragraphs arguing for a specific furniture arrangement with reasons based on function, space utilization, and aesthetics. Classification activities require written explanations of sorting decisions, pushing students to articulate their reasoning in complete sentences. The furniture theme is particularly powerful for second graders because it makes geometry feel practical and purposeful rather than abstract, showing children that the measurement concepts they are learning in math class describe the real objects they sit on, sleep in, and organize their lives around every day.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Calculate perimeter of rectangular furniture surfaces and solve two-step measurement word problems using standard units', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read informational passages about furniture design, materials, and history and compare information across sources', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Create scaled room layouts on grid paper and write analytical descriptions explaining design choices with spatial reasoning', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the spatial visualization skills to work with scaled representations of rooms, understanding that grid squares represent real-world units. Their measurement abilities allow them to use rulers accurately and record data in organized tables. The ability to compose multi-paragraph writing means they can produce room descriptions and design explanations that demonstrate both spatial understanding and communication skills simultaneously.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Have students measure three pieces of classroom furniture with rulers, record the length and width of each surface, then calculate the perimeter by adding all sides, providing concrete area and perimeter introduction through familiar objects.',\n        'Challenge students to design their dream bedroom on centimeter grid paper with a fixed room size, requiring them to fit specific furniture items within the space while explaining their arrangement choices in a written paragraph.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade furniture worksheets introduce area and perimeter?', answer: 'Rectangular furniture surfaces like table tops, desks, and rugs provide perfect concrete models for area and perimeter. Students measure the length and width of furniture items, add all four sides to find perimeter, and count grid squares covering the surface to explore area. These hands-on calculations make abstract geometry concepts tangible and personally meaningful.' },\n        { question: 'What spatial reasoning skills do second-grade furniture worksheets develop beyond first grade?', answer: 'They advance from describing positions with prepositions to creating scaled room plans on grid paper, calculating whether furniture fits within specific dimensions, and analyzing spatial efficiency. Students develop the ability to translate between three-dimensional spaces and two-dimensional representations, a critical skill for geometry and map reading.' },\n        { question: 'How do furniture worksheets support second-grade writing standards?', answer: 'Students write multi-paragraph room descriptions using precise measurement vocabulary, compose persuasive paragraphs defending furniture arrangement choices with evidence-based reasoning, and produce informational text about furniture materials and design. This variety of writing purposes builds the organized, evidence-supported writing that second-grade standards demand.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'body': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring genuine scientific curiosity, independent reading fluency, and multi-digit math skills to body-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect human anatomy to health data tracking, informational reading about body systems, and organized scientific writing. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, measure using standard units, and read multi-paragraph informational text independently, making them ready for body activities that go well beyond naming parts into understanding how systems work together. Math worksheets at this level use health and body data for authentic computation: if your resting heart rate is seventy-two beats per minute and after exercise it rises to one hundred and eight, how much did it increase, or if you need eight glasses of water per day and have had five so far, how many more do you need. Measurement activities involve recording actual body measurements like height, arm span, and hand length in inches and centimeters, then organizing data in tables and comparing measurements across classmates. Reading passages explore how the skeletal system provides structure, how the digestive system breaks down food into energy, or how the respiratory system delivers oxygen to muscles, with comprehension questions that require identifying main ideas, tracing processes through multiple steps, and drawing conclusions from scientific information. Writing tasks challenge students to compose informational paragraphs about a body system, opinion pieces about why a particular health habit is important with supporting evidence, or procedural descriptions of how the body performs a function like digestion or breathing. Scientific vocabulary expands significantly at this level to include terms like vertebrae, oxygen, nutrients, circulatory, and ligament, building the domain-specific language that supports science literacy. The body theme is uniquely powerful for second graders because the subject of study is always present and personally relevant, transforming every worksheet into a tool for understanding themselves better while building the academic skills they need for increasingly rigorous schoolwork.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve two-step word problems within 100 using body measurements, health data, and anatomical quantities', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph informational text about body systems and trace processes from input to output', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write organized informational and opinion paragraphs about health topics using scientific vocabulary and supporting evidence', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the scientific thinking to understand that body systems have inputs, processes, and outputs, such as food entering the digestive system and energy being the result. Their measurement skills allow them to collect and organize real body data, and their writing abilities support multi-sentence explanations of biological processes. The growing interest in how things work internally makes body system exploration genuinely exciting at this age.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Have students measure their own height, arm span, and hand length in centimeters, record the data in a class table, then calculate differences and answer comparison questions, connecting body themes directly to measurement and data standards.',\n        'Assign a body system research project where students read about one system, create a labeled diagram, and write an informational paragraph explaining what the system does and why it matters for overall health.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade body worksheets connect to science standards?', answer: 'They address life science standards by exploring body systems and their functions, including how the skeletal system provides support, how muscles enable movement, and how the digestive system processes food. Students trace biological processes through multiple steps and use scientific vocabulary like nutrients, oxygen, and circulatory in context, building the science literacy that standards require.' },\n        { question: 'What measurement and data skills do second-grade body worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students measure body dimensions using rulers and tape measures in standard units, record data in organized tables, compare measurements across multiple items, and calculate differences between values. This authentic data collection and analysis directly addresses second-grade measurement and data standards while making statistics feel personally relevant.' },\n        { question: 'How do body worksheets support second-grade informational writing?', answer: 'Students compose structured paragraphs about body systems with topic sentences, scientific facts as supporting details, and concluding statements. They write opinion pieces about health habits backed by evidence from their reading. This combination of scientific content with organized paragraph writing builds both content knowledge and the compositional skills that second-grade standards emphasize.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'emotions': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring the reading comprehension, analytical reasoning, and writing fluency needed to engage with emotions worksheets at a genuinely transformative level, moving from identifying feelings to analyzing emotional situations, evaluating coping strategies, and writing thoughtfully about the complexity of human emotional experience. Seven- and eight-year-olds can read multi-paragraph passages independently, construct organized written responses, and think critically about why people feel differently in the same situation, making them ready for social-emotional learning activities that develop both academic skills and emotional intelligence simultaneously. Reading activities present longer narrative scenarios where characters face realistic social dilemmas, such as being excluded from a game, receiving criticism on their work, or witnessing a friend being treated unfairly, and comprehension questions require students to identify multiple emotions a character might feel, analyze why different characters react differently, and evaluate which coping strategies would be most effective. Math activities use emotion data at a more sophisticated level, presenting survey results about how students in a school feel about different situations and asking children to interpret bar graphs, calculate differences, and draw evidence-based conclusions about emotional patterns. Vocabulary expands dramatically to include nuanced words like conflicted, empathetic, resilient, overwhelmed, grateful, and apprehensive, giving students precision instruments for describing their inner world. Writing tasks ask students to compose personal narrative paragraphs about a time they experienced a strong emotion, opinion pieces about which coping strategy works best and why, or character analysis paragraphs explaining why a story character made a particular choice. The second-grade level is a pivotal moment for emotional development because children are forming the self-regulation habits and social reasoning patterns that will define their approach to relationships throughout elementary school and beyond, making these worksheets an investment in both academic achievement and lifelong emotional wellbeing.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Analyze multi-character scenarios to identify different emotional perspectives and evaluate appropriate responses', area: 'social' },\n        { skill: 'Read narrative passages about emotional situations and write character analysis paragraphs with evidence from the text', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Interpret emotion-related survey data from graphs and charts and draw evidence-based conclusions about patterns', area: 'math' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed sufficient theory of mind to understand that the same event can produce different emotions in different people based on their perspectives and experiences. Their writing abilities allow them to articulate nuanced emotional reasoning in organized paragraphs. Socially, they are increasingly aware of group dynamics, fairness, and social norms, making scenario-based worksheets about inclusion, conflict, and empathy both timely and deeply engaging.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Present a scenario with two characters who feel differently about the same event and have students write a paragraph from each character\\\'s perspective, building empathy and perspective-taking through structured writing practice.',\n        'Create a class emotion survey where students anonymously report how they feel about different school situations, then use the aggregated data in a math lesson about interpreting bar graphs and discussing what the patterns reveal about shared experiences.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade emotions worksheets differ from earlier grades in academic rigor?', answer: 'They move from identification to analysis and evaluation. Instead of naming an emotion, students analyze why characters feel differently about the same event, evaluate multiple coping strategies and argue for the most effective one in writing, and interpret emotion data from graphs. Reading passages are multi-paragraph narratives requiring inference, and writing tasks demand organized paragraphs with evidence.' },\n        { question: 'Can emotions worksheets help second graders navigate bullying and exclusion?', answer: 'Yes. Scenario-based worksheets present realistic situations involving exclusion, teasing, and unfairness in a structured format that allows students to analyze the emotions involved, consider multiple perspectives, and evaluate response strategies before encountering similar situations in real life. This rehearsal-on-paper approach builds both emotional vocabulary and practical coping skills.' },\n        { question: 'How do second-grade emotions worksheets integrate with academic writing standards?', answer: 'Students write personal narratives about emotional experiences with clear beginning, middle, and end structure. They compose opinion paragraphs arguing for specific coping strategies with supporting reasons. They produce character analysis paragraphs using text evidence. This range of writing purposes directly addresses second-grade narrative, opinion, and informational writing standards.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'jobs': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring multi-digit arithmetic skills, independent reading fluency, and growing awareness of the wider world to jobs-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect career exploration to real-world math involving money and scheduling, informational reading about diverse professions, and organized writing about community systems. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, work with time and money concepts, and write structured paragraphs, making them ready for workplace-themed activities that go far beyond simple matching into genuine analysis of how careers contribute to society. Math worksheets at this level present problems like a firefighter works a twelve-hour shift starting at seven in the morning, what time does the shift end, or a farmer harvests forty-eight bushels of apples on Monday and sixty-three on Tuesday, how many bushels were harvested in total. Money math enters through workplace scenarios: if a baker sells muffins for two dollars each and sells thirty-five in a morning, estimate the total earnings. Reading activities include multi-paragraph career profiles that describe not just what a worker does but what education and training the job requires, what challenges the role involves, and how it connects to other careers in the community. Comprehension questions require students to compare two careers, identify similarities and differences, and draw conclusions about what makes different jobs important. Writing tasks ask students to compose informational paragraphs about a career they researched, opinion pieces about which community job they consider most essential and why, or interview-style reports after talking to a family member about their work. Career web activities map how multiple jobs connect in chains of production and service, building systems thinking that extends beyond individual roles to community-wide interdependence. The combination of authentic workplace math, informational literacy, and social studies content makes jobs worksheets a powerfully integrated second-grade resource that helps children see education itself as preparation for meaningful contribution to their communities.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve two-step word problems within 100 involving workplace quantities, money estimation, and scheduling with time', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read multi-paragraph career profiles and compare different professions based on training, tools, responsibilities, and community impact', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Write organized informational and opinion paragraphs about careers using evidence gathered from reading and research', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed enough abstract reasoning to understand that careers require preparation and training, not just desire. Their math skills support working with time on clocks and money in dollars, both of which are central to workplace concepts. Their growing social awareness means they are genuinely curious about how communities are organized and why different people do different work, making career exploration intellectually stimulating rather than purely aspirational.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Have students interview a family member about their job using a structured question worksheet, then write a career profile paragraph summarizing what they learned, connecting oral research to informational writing practice.',\n        'Create a classroom economy where students hold different job roles for a week, earning and spending classroom currency, then write a reflection about what they learned about their role\\\'s responsibilities and challenges.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade jobs worksheets incorporate money math?', answer: 'They present workplace scenarios involving earning, spending, and making change with dollar amounts. Problems like a store clerk sells items totaling sixty-seven dollars and the customer pays with a hundred dollar bill require subtraction within 100. Price estimation, total calculation, and budget comparison all connect career contexts to the money and arithmetic skills second-grade standards emphasize.' },\n        { question: 'What research and writing skills do second-grade jobs worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students gather information from career profiles and interviews, then organize their findings into structured paragraphs with topic sentences and supporting details. They write opinion pieces arguing which community job is most important and defend their position with evidence. This research-to-writing process builds the informational literacy skills that second-grade standards require across all subjects.' },\n        { question: 'How do jobs worksheets support second-grade social studies standards?', answer: 'They address standards on community organization, civic responsibility, and economic concepts by mapping how different jobs serve community needs, exploring how workers depend on each other, and introducing basic economic ideas like goods, services, supply, and demand. Career web activities build systems thinking about how communities function as interconnected networks of specialized roles.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'music': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring the mathematical maturity, reading independence, and creative sophistication needed to engage with music worksheets at a level that genuinely connects musical concepts to rigorous academic content across multiple subjects. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, understand multiplication as repeated groups, and read multi-paragraph informational text independently, making them ready for music activities that explore rhythm mathematics, instrument science, and music history with real depth. Math worksheets at this level use musical concepts for authentic computation: if a rhythm pattern repeats every four beats and the song has thirty-two beats, how many times does the pattern repeat. Time signature concepts introduce fractions naturally, as students discover that four quarter notes fill one measure and two half notes do the same, building fraction equivalence understanding through auditory and visual experience. Growing patterns advance significantly, with students analyzing sequences where the number of beats per measure increases by two each time and predicting what the tenth measure would contain. Reading passages explore how sound is produced by vibrating strings, air columns, and membranes, connecting music to physical science concepts. Other passages introduce music traditions from different cultures, explaining how instruments like the djembe, sitar, and pan flute reflect the materials and traditions of their regions. Writing tasks challenge students to compose informational paragraphs about an instrument family, opinion pieces about which instrument they would most like to learn and why, or descriptive paragraphs about how a piece of music makes them feel using precise emotional and musical vocabulary. The synthesis of mathematical pattern thinking, scientific understanding of sound, cultural awareness, and creative expression makes music worksheets an extraordinarily rich second-grade resource that develops the whole child while meeting rigorous academic standards.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve multiplication-readiness problems using rhythmic grouping, beat counting, and pattern repetition within musical contexts', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Read informational passages about sound science, instrument construction, and world music traditions and summarize key concepts', area: 'literacy' },\n        { skill: 'Compose organized paragraphs expressing musical opinions and describing how music connects to math, science, and cultural traditions', area: 'cognitive' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the abstract reasoning to understand that musical patterns follow mathematical rules that can be described, predicted, and extended. Their growing scientific curiosity means they are fascinated by how instruments produce different sounds, and their cultural awareness is expanding to include genuine interest in how people in other parts of the world create music. Writing fluency at this level supports multi-paragraph compositions that integrate musical knowledge with personal response.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Use rhythm patterns to teach multiplication as repeated groups: four measures with three beats each gives twelve beats total, making the abstract concept of equal groups tangible through clapping and counting.',\n        'Have students research one instrument from a non-Western tradition, read about its cultural significance, and write an informational paragraph that includes how it is made, how it produces sound, and where it is traditionally played.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade music worksheets connect to multiplication readiness?', answer: 'Musical measures naturally organize beats into equal groups, which is the foundational concept behind multiplication. When students calculate that four measures of three beats each equals twelve total beats, they are practicing repeated addition and equal-group thinking. Rhythm worksheets provide a creative, auditory context for the abstract mathematical concept of multiplying.' },\n        { question: 'What science connections do second-grade music worksheets offer?', answer: 'They explore how sound is produced through vibration of strings, air columns, and stretched membranes. Students read about why a short string produces a higher pitch than a long string, how drum size affects sound depth, and why different materials create different tonal qualities. These connections address physical science standards on sound and vibration.' },\n        { question: 'How do music worksheets build cultural awareness at the second-grade level?', answer: 'Passages about world music traditions introduce instruments and musical styles from diverse cultures, including African drumming, Indian classical music, East Asian flutes, and Latin American percussion. Students learn that music is a universal human activity expressed differently across cultures, building respect for diversity while expanding their knowledge of global traditions.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,

  'toys': `    },\n    'second-grade': {\n      intro: 'Second graders bring multi-digit arithmetic fluency, measurement skills, and growing critical thinking to toy-themed worksheets, enabling activities that connect beloved play objects to two-step word problems, standard unit measurement, data analysis from surveys, and organized opinion writing. Seven- and eight-year-olds can add and subtract within one hundred, measure with rulers, interpret graphs, and write structured paragraphs, making them ready for toy activities that transform casual play into rigorous academic learning. Math worksheets at this level present multi-step problems like a toy store has ninety-four action figures, sells twenty-seven on Saturday and thirty-one on Sunday, how many are left on the shelf Monday morning. Equal sharing problems introduce division readiness: if three friends want to share twenty-four building blocks equally, how many does each friend get. Price comparison grows more complex as students compare the total cost of different toy combinations, determine how much more one set costs than another, and calculate whether they can afford a purchase with a given budget. Measurement activities have students use rulers to measure actual toy dimensions in inches and centimeters, record data in organized tables, and compare lengths, heights, and widths across multiple objects. Reading passages explore topics like how classic toys were invented, why certain toys become popular, or how toy designers use engineering principles, with comprehension questions requiring main idea identification and evidence-based reasoning across paragraphs. Writing tasks challenge students to compose persuasive toy reviews with specific reasons supporting their opinion, informational paragraphs about how a toy is made, or comparison paragraphs analyzing two toys across multiple attributes. Data interpretation worksheets present bar graphs and pictographs from toy preference surveys and ask multi-step questions about totals, differences, and patterns. The toy theme maintains its unique motivational power at the second-grade level because children still care deeply about their toys and collections, making every mathematical calculation and reading passage feel personally important in a way that abstract exercises rarely achieve.',\n      objectives: [\n        { skill: 'Solve two-step word problems within 100 involving toy quantities, prices, and equal sharing for division readiness', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Measure toy objects using standard units, record data in tables, and compare measurements across multiple items', area: 'math' },\n        { skill: 'Write organized opinion paragraphs reviewing toys and informational paragraphs about toy design using evidence and specific details', area: 'literacy' },\n      ],\n      developmentalNotes: 'Second graders have developed the mathematical reasoning to work through two-step problems where the answer from the first operation becomes the input for the second, and toy scenarios make tracking these quantities feel natural. Their measurement skills support using rulers independently, and their writing has matured enough to produce organized paragraphs with topic sentences, multiple supporting details, and concluding statements that communicate a clear point about a toy they care about.',\n      teachingTips: [\n        'Set up a toy measurement station where students measure five different classroom toys in both inches and centimeters, record both measurements in a data table, and then answer comparison questions about which toy is longest, which has the greatest difference between inch and centimeter values, and what patterns they notice.',\n        'Have students write persuasive toy reviews modeled on real product reviews, including a star rating, three specific reasons for their opinion, and a recommendation about who would enjoy the toy, developing opinion writing within an authentic format they encounter online.',\n      ],\n      faq: [\n        { question: 'How do second-grade toy worksheets prepare students for multiplication and division?', answer: 'Equal sharing problems like dividing twenty-four toys among three friends build the partitioning concept that underlies division. Repeated group problems like four bags with six toys each develop multiplication readiness through addition of equal groups. Toy contexts make these abstract operations concrete because children intuitively understand fair sharing and group counting with their own collections.' },\n        { question: 'What data and measurement skills do second-grade toy worksheets develop?', answer: 'Students measure real toys with rulers in standard units, record measurements in organized data tables, and compare values across items. They interpret bar graphs and pictographs from toy surveys, calculate totals and differences between categories, and draw conclusions about patterns. These activities directly address the measurement and data strand of second-grade math standards.' },\n        { question: 'How do toy worksheets support second-grade opinion writing?', answer: 'Students write structured toy reviews with a clear opinion statement, three or more supporting reasons based on specific toy attributes, and a concluding recommendation. This authentic writing format, modeled on real product reviews, teaches organized opinion writing with evidence in a context children find genuinely motivating and personally relevant.' },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n\n  // -- FAQ --`,
};

let successCount = 0;

for (const [theme, block] of Object.entries(themes)) {
  const filePath = path.join(BASE, theme, 'en.ts');
  let content = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');
  const idx = content.indexOf(MARKER);
  if (idx === -1) {
    console.error('Pattern not found in ' + theme);
    process.exit(1);
  }
  content = content.substring(0, idx) + block + content.substring(idx + MARKER.length);
  fs.writeFileSync(filePath, content, 'utf8');
  console.log('Updated ' + theme);
  successCount++;
}

console.log('\nDone! Updated ' + successCount + ' theme files.');
